.Im MicroEmacs variable|buffer|macrodevelop
.so me.tni
.TH $buffer-encoding 5
.XI $buffer-encoding - "Buffer file encoding"
.SH NAME
.na
$buffer-encoding \- Buffer file encoding
.Me $a
.ad
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB$buffer-encoding\fR "\fIencoding\fR"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B $buffer-encoding
is the encoding of the current buffer's file, the name of a single byte code page defined by
.Ht set-char-mask 2
flag \fBC\fR, normally with
.Ht code-page-define 3 ,
or in a Unicode build one of:
.IP \fButf8\fR
UTF-8.
.IP "\fButf16le\fR, \fButf16be\fR"
UTF-16, little or big endian.
.IP "\fButf32le\fR, \fButf32be\fR"
UTF-32, little or big endian.
.PP
followed by \fC-bom\fR when the file starts with a byte order mark, e.g. \fCutf16le-bom\fR. When set the code page
must exist, the names are case sensitive; a code page name cannot start with \fCutf\fR so the two never clash.
.PP
A Unicode build detects the encoding when reading a file, a file which is not Unicode being read with the current
code page, and writes the file back in the buffer's encoding, so a file stays byte identical. Setting a different
encoding converts the file when next saved; a re-read of the file then uses it rather than detecting the encoding
again, so a file read with the wrong code page can be fixed by setting the right one and re-reading it. A new buffer
is \fCutf8\fR.
.PP
Other builds set it to the code page current when the file was loaded, \fC""\fR for a new buffer; setting it
changes the code page recorded but the text is not converted, an empty value is allowed.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
.Ht set-char-mask 2 ,
.Ht code-page-define 3 ,
.Ht change-buffer-encoding 3 ,
.Ht reread-file 3 .
.ad
.FH
